[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Restart / durability protocol
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Keep invoking `./jobs reindex` until it exits 0 (75 means the supervised budget
[?2004l[?2004h> expired; committed work is safe). `./jobs status` reports checkpoint-certified
[?2004l[?2004h> rows, excluding any uncommitted tail. `./jobs history` records supervised runs.
[?2004l[?2004h> The usual budget, grace period, cooldown, and supervisor locking are unchanged.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Each batch (or partial batch on SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP) is committed in this order:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Flush rows, fsync **every** touched shard (including rotated ones), and sync
[?2004l[?2004h>    the index directory.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Write the new checkpoint to a temporary file, fsync it, atomically replace
[?2004l[?2004h>    `var/checkpoint.json`, then sync its directory. The checkpoint includes the
[?2004l[?2004h>    last processed ID, document count, completion flag, and shard byte lengths.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. On restart, truncate tails to those lengths and remove uncommitted shards
[?2004l[?2004h>    before resuming strictly **after** the last processed ID. SIGKILL during any
[?2004l[?2004h>    stage can leave replayable work, but cannot advance progress ahead of data.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Missing/truncated committed shards are reported as storage damage rather than
[?2004l[?2004h> silently skipped. Old checkpoints without shard lengths are not trustworthy
[?2004l[?2004h> (the old worker checkpointed buffered rows), so they trigger a one-time rebuild.
[?2004l[?2004h> The document transform and JSONL row format are unchanged. This is a resumable
[?2004l[?2004h> full backfill over a stable corpus, not an incremental change/deletion feed;
[?2004l[?2004h> use `./jobs clean` to intentionally start a new full backfill after source changes.
[?2004l[?2004h> Do not clean a paused run if you want to preserve its progress.
